## 1. Introduction
In contemporary world, fossil fuels (such as coal, petroleum, natural gas, etc.) are over exploited, leading to serious environmental pollution and energy crisis. Therefore, it’s urging to search renewable energy which can replace fossil fuels [1–3]. With the cleanliness and economic properties, solar energy is considered as one of the most promising candidate renewable energy [4], and it is of great significance to develop and utilize solar energy. Storing solar energy in the form of hydrogen is considered one of the best ways to develop clean energy [5]. As an environmentfriendly material, photocatalyst makes use of solar energy to produce hydrogen energy and is expected to solve the environmental problems and energy crisis [6–8]. As photocatalysis technology decompose water resources into hydrogen and oxygen continuously, it has attracted widespread interest due to it low cost and eco-friendly economic potential [9].
The cocatalyst supported on the surface of the photocatalyst affects the reaction process of photogenerated electrons and holes on the surface of the photocatalyst. Li Can et al. [10] believed that the supported catalyst could promote the redox reaction by providing an active site and inhibiting recombination of photogenerated charges. The most important is the cocatalyst makes the photocatalytic reaction proceed by lowering the activation energy. Therefore, supporting cocatalyst is an important method to increase the photocatalytic activity of the photocatalyst. However, noble metal-containing cocatalysts which are expensive still exist mainly as the most effective promoters [11]. Therefore, the development of low-cost non-noble metal cocatalyst is the urgent task of reducing the cost of hydrogen production. Among them, metal sulfides as cocatalyst can effectively improve the photocatalytic hydrogen production efficiency [12,13].
Graphene is a kind of periodic honeycomb two-dimensional new nano-material formed by hexagonal arrangement of $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ hybrid carbon atoms, whose thickness is only $0.335\mathrm{nm}$ [14]. In 2004, Novoselov et al. [15] first made graphene. The theoretical specific surface area of graphene is up to $2600\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ [16] and possesses outstanding thermal conductivity and mechanical properties [17], especially the high electron mobility at room temperature [18]. In addition, it has a series of properties such as half-integer hall effect, unique quantum tunneling effect and bipolar electric field effect [19–23]. In particular, it has excellent electrical conductivity and large specific surface area, which provides a viable approach for solving the bottleneck problem in the photocatalytic reaction.
Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs) are currently attracting the attention of researchers. It is a kind of novel porous material with an infinite network structure, which is connected with metal ions or metal clusters by ligands. It is easy to get and possess some advantages such as rich structure, high porosity and ratio surface area, etc. In recent years, MOFs has been widely used in the field of photocatalysis. On the one hand, the porosity makes MOFs have more exposed active sites and channels, which facilitate the transmission of charge, thereby contributing to reducing the compound of the electron-hole in the bulk phase and improving its utilization efficiency. On the other hand, MOFs can further broaden its light absorption ability through compounding with other materials, and forming hybrid structure to accelerate the transfer and separation of photogenerated carriers, so as to further improve the photocatalytic activity [24–26].
In this work, an efficient photocatalytic hydrogen production system was constructed with Eosin-Y as photosensitizer, TEOA as electron donor and $\Gamma{\sf G O}/{\sf M O F}/{\sf N i}_{4}S_{3}$ compound as photocatalyst. The Uio-66 $(\mathrm{Zr})/\mathrm{r}{\mathsf{G}}0$ grew on graphene by the solvothermal method, then the above product was combined with $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ by hydrothermal method to prepare $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ photocatalyst. The structure and photoelectric properties of the composites were characterized by a variety of techniques, and the performance of photocatalytic hydrogen production under visible light of composite materials is studied. The fluorescence spectroscopy analysis indicated that the introduction of $\mathsf{N i}_{4}\mathsf{S}_{3}$ enhanced the efficiency of charge separation and electron transfer, which significantly enhanced the photocatalytic hydrogen evolution activity of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{GO}}/$ $\mathsf{M O F}/\mathsf{N i}_{4}S_{3}$ in the photosensitization system.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Photocatalyst preparation
## 2.1.1. Preparation of reduced graphene oxide/Metal-Organic Framework (rGO/MOF)
All chemicals used in this work were analytical grade and used as received without further purification. In detail, $10\mathrm{mg}$ of oxidized graphite was dispersed in $50\mathrm{ml}$ of N,N- dimethylformamide (DMF), then 1 mmol of $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ and 1 mmol of terephthalic acid were added and the mixture was magnetically stirred for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The above solution was transferred to $100\mathrm{ml}$ Polytetrafluoroethylene high-pressure reactor with $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ , then naturally cooled to room temperature. Next, the synthetics were washed three times with DMF and methanol, respectively. Finally, the product was dried overnight in oven at the temperature of $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ .
## 2.1.2. Synthesis of $r G O/M O F/N i_{4}S_{3}$ composite photocatalyst
The preparation process of rGO/MOF $\ensuremath{\vert\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}}$ composite photocatalyst was as follows: First, $_{0.1\mathrm{~g~}}$ of rGO/MOF photocatalyst was dispersed into $20\mathrm{ml}$ of distilled water at room temperature to form a homogeneous system. Next, the nickel acetate was added to the dispersion, and after stirring for 1 h, $40\mathrm{ml}$ of a thiourea solution (containing $_{2\mathrm{~g~}}$ of thiourea) was added to the dispersion and stirred for $^{1\mathrm{{h}}}$ . The resulting homogeneous solution was transferred to a $100\mathrm{ml}$ polytetrafluoroethylene autoclave and held at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ . After cooled to the room temperature, it was filtered and washed with distilled water and ethanol, and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight.
In the typical synthesis of the $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ photocatalyst, the different weight ratios of nickel acetate solution which has occupied rGO/MOF were controlled at $10\mathrm{{wt\%}}$ , $15\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $20\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $25\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $30\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively. For comparison, pure $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ photocatalyst were also be prepared using the same method as follows: $_{0.02\mathrm{g}}$ of nickel acetate was dispersed in $20\mathrm{ml}$ of distilled water for $30\mathrm{min}$ to obtain a homogeneous dispersion, and then $40\mathrm{ml}$ of thiourea solution (containing $_{2\mathrm{~g~}}$ of thiourea) was added to the above dispersion and stirred for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The resulting homogeneous solution was transferred to a $100\mathrm{ml}$ polytetrafluoroethylene autoclave and held at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ . After cooled to the room temperature, it was filtered and washed with distilled water and ethanol, and dried at $60^{\circ}C$ overnight.
## 2.2. Characterization
A JEOL JSM-6701F scanning electron microscope was performed to observe the morphologies and microstructures of specimens, with which the accelerating voltage of $5\mathrm{kV}$ was adopt. The sample was dropped on a copper tray. Given the poor conductivity of the sample, the sample was sprayed with gold. The FEI Tecnai TF20 high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (USA; equipped with an energy dispersive X-ray analyzer (EDXA); accelerating voltage of $200\mathrm{kV},$ was used to characterize the high resolution transmission electron microscope of the photocatalyst. The chemical element composition of the specimen was determined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, ESCALAB 250Xi). The phase compositions of the photocatalysts were analyzed with an X-ray diffraction (XRD; Philips, Netherlands, Cu-Ka radiation, current $150\mathrm{mA},$ voltage $40\mathrm{kV}.$ ). Fluorescence Spectroscopy (PL) was analyzed using a FluoroMAX-4 spectrometer (HORIBA Scientific, France). A UV-2550 (Shimadzu) spectrometer was conducted to measure the UV–visible diffuse reflectance (bandgap). The photoelectrochemical tests were executed via an electrochemical workstation (PAR VersaSTAT 4) with a standard three-electrode system. The system consists of saturated calomel electrode (SCE) as reference electrode, the Pt electrode as a counter electrode and working electrode with the soaking area of $1{\mathrm{cm}}^{2}$ . $\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{S O}_{4}$ aqueous solution $(0.1\mathrm{mol}\bullet\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ and $15\%(\mathrm{v/v)}$ TEOA aqueous solution $\mathrm{\boldmath~\pH=9~}$ were used as electrolyte. The light source is $300\mathrm{W}$ xenon lamp with $420\mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter to filter out ultraviolet light.
## 2.3. Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution experiments
Photocatalytic hydrogen production experiments were carried out in a quartz reaction flask $(62~\mathrm{ml})$ by a Prefectlight PCX50A multipass light photocatalytic reaction system. Specifically, $10\mathrm{mg}$ of photocatalyst powder was added to $30\mathrm{ml}$ TEOA ( $15\%$ , TEOA as electronic donors). After $10\mathrm{min}$ of ultrasonic dispersion, $20\mathrm{mg}$ of Eosin Y was added to the system with continuously magnetic stirring. Before irradiation, the system was displaced for $30\mathrm{min}$ by high purity $\Nu_{2}$ . The photocatalyst was kept in a suspended state by magnetic stirring. To measure the amount of hydrogen evolution, $0.5~\mathrm{ml}$ of gas was extracted from the reaction bottle every $30\mathrm{min}$ to analyze the amount of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ generated by using gas chromatography (Tianmei GC7900, TCD, 13X packed column, $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ as carrier) and using the external standard method to quantify.
The turnover number (TON) of dye Eosin Y for $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution is calculated with the following equation:
2 x number of evolved hydrogen molecules TON number of dye Eosin Y molecules
In this paper, the moles of dye Eosin Y is $2.9\times10^{-5}$ in the reaction solution and the amount of production $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ is $280\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ in $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ over $\mathrm{EY-rGO/MOF/Ni_{4}S_{3}}$ $(20\%)$ photocatalyst. Therefore, the calculated TON is 19.37 of dye Eosin Y in $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ for photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution using the above equation.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. The characterization of $r G O/M O F/N i_{4}S_{3}$ composite photocatalyst
Fig. 1 manifested the XRD patterns of pure rGO/MOF, pure $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ni}_{4}}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ , and $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ composite photocatalysts. The characteristic diffraction peak (JCPDS#52-1027) of $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ is strong and sharp, indicating that it possessed a high degree of crystallinity. The peak of pure rGO/MOF has also performed good crystallinity which was consistent with the reported results [27]. The characteristic peaks of pure rGO/MOF and pure $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ can be both observed in the XRD pattern of $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ composite photocatalyst. With the raise of pure $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ content, the intensity of the characteristic diffraction peak in the $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ composite photocatalyst has gradually decreased with no other impurity peak appeared. The above XRD results showed that the pure rGO/MOF and the pure $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ni}_{4}}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ has formed $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ composite photocatalyst.
Fig. 2(a) and (b) are SEM images of uio-66 and $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ composite photocatalysts. It can be easily seen that the pure uio-66 (Fig. 2a) was irregular nanoparticles with multilayered structure. The $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ (Fig. 2b) indicated that there are some irregular particles on the surface of the graphene sheet, namely uio-66 and $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ nanoparticles, which distributed on the surface of graphene and grew into irregular particles. The size of these irregular particles are about $10{-}100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which are distributed evenly. Fig. 2(c) and (d) are TEM images of uio-66 and rGO/uio-66. As can be seen in Fig. 2(d), a large number of uio-66 nanoparticles grew on the surface of the graphene sheet. Fig. 2(e) clearly manifested that the addition of $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ nanoparticles resulted in the aggregation of $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ particles on the rGO/MOF surface in the $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ complex. It can be more clearly seen from HRTEM image (Fig. 2e). The attached nanocrystals of $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ have the lattice fringe spacing of $0.184\mathrm{nm}$ and $0.157{\mathrm{nm}}$ , which can be indexed to the (2 2 0) and (3 1 1) planes according to JCPDS#52-1207. The results of EDS (Fig. 2f) showed that the C, O, Zr, Ni and S elements coexisted in the $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ nanocomposite, which is in accord with the result of following XPS characterization.
## 3.2. Photocatalytic $H_{2}$ production activity
As shown in Fig. 3, we have tested the photocatalytic hydrogen production activity of the pure $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ photocatalyst, rGO/MOF photocatalyst and $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ composite photocatalyst with different percentage content of Ni under the visible light irradiation. It can be seen from the figure that the photocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{EY-rGO/MOF/Ni_{4}S_{3}}$ is significantly better than of pure $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ and pure rGO/MOF photocatalysts. Under visible light irradiation, pure $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ and pure rGO/MOF photocatalysts showed a very low hydrogen production amount of only $20.32\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ and $1.51\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ respectively. The reason is due to its own poor photogenerated electron-hole separation efficiency, which is not conducive to proton reduction on its surface. The hydrogen production amount of EY-sensitized $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ photocatalyst has reached $280\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ , which is about 14 times than pure $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ catalyst and 185 times than pure rGO/MOF photocatalyst. This may be related to $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ adsorption and electron transfer efficiency. The process of hydrogen production generally through three processes: $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ adsorption, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ reduction and H atom desorption. The adsorption of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ on the surface of cocatalyst is the key factor in the process of hydrogen production, which has determined the rate of H atom adsorption and desorption and the formation of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ on the photocatalyst surface.
Fig. 4(b) showed the effect of EY concentration on the photocatalytic activity. The hydrogen production of EY-sensitized $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{GO}}/$ $\mathsf{M O F}/\mathsf{N i}_{4}\mathsf{S}_{3}$ system has enhanced with the increase of EY concentration when the EY concentrations is less than $1.0\times10^{-4}\mathrm{M}$ ; While when the EY concentration was lower than $1.0\times10^{-4}\mathrm{M}$ , it has decreased with the increase of EY concentration. The above phenomenon may be due to the shielding effect of free dyes. At the lower concentrations of EY, the dye adsorbed concentration on the surface of the photocatalyst has also raised, which was advantageous for increasing the absorption efficiency of dye on the photocatalyst surface and the injection of photogenerated electrons into the semiconductor surface under visible light irradiation. Therefore, the hydrogen production activity will improve when increasing the Eosin-Y concentration at lower concentrations.
However, when the concentration of EY was too high, not only the number of photons irradiated to the surface of the catalyst was reduced, but also the free-state EY in the system was excited contributing to absorbing most of the incident light and easily quenched, thereby reducing the transfer rate of photogenerated electron. Consequently, the efficiency of photocatalytic hydrogen production was greatly limited. At the same time, the concentration quenching effect was also one of the important reasons which lead to the above phenomenon. When the concentration of EY was too high, the excited dye species would collide with another base or excited dye species and resulting in the deactivation of the excited dye through non-radiative transitions, radiative transitions, and intersystem transitions.
## 3.3. Analysis of optical properties
We studied the mechanism of action of $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ in $_{\mathrm{EY-rGO/MOF/}}$ $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ni}_{4}}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ system on the electron transfer and photocatalytic hydrogen production activity by steady-state and transient fluorescence spectroscopy. As shown in Fig. 5, the Eosin-Y solution (1  106 $\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ was excited by light at the wavelength of $480\mathrm{nm}$ , and a typical broad emission peak appeared at a position of $537\mathrm{nm}$ . With the addition of pure rGO/MOF, the intensity of the emission peak decreased significantly. When $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ was added, the fluorescence intensity reduced sharply, indicating that the recombination of photogenerated electron-hole pairs was greatly suppressed due to the formation of excellent electron acceptor$\mathsf{N i}_{4}\mathsf{S}_{3}$ and highly hybridized nanostructures, thereby improved the photo-generated electron transfer efficiency and photocatalytic hydrogen production efficiency.
For further discuss the interaction between the excited state EY and the photocatalyst, we used transient fluorescence spectroscopy to characterize the fluorescence quenching behavior of excited EY in the presence of $\mathrm{EY-rGO/MOF/Ni_{4}S_{3}}$ composites. The data obtained after fitting are shown in Table 1. It can be seen that the fluorescence decay of EY in TEOA solution can be described by single-exponential fluorescence decay kinetics, indicating that there is one luminescent species with a lifetime of 0.226 ns under excitation. The fluorescence decay time of EY remained unchanged by and large when $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ and Uio-66 were added to EY solution respectively. The above phenomenon indicated that fluorescence quenching of excited EY followed the static quenching mechanism in the presence of $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ and Uio-66, showing that EY adsorbed on the surface of $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ and Uio-66 respectively, and formed the ground state complexes $\mathrm{'}\mathrm{EY-Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ and EY-Uio-66) with weak fluorescence emission properties. When rGO/MOF was added to EY solution, the life of EY was enhanced and the fluorescence decay of EY needed to be described by double exponential fluorescence decay kinetics. When $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ was added to EY solution, the lifetime of EY has been sharply increased. The results showed that the $\Gamma{\sf G O}/{\sf M O F}/{\sf N i}_{4}S_{3}$ effectively prolonged the life of the excited-state EY, which is conducive to the photocatalytic hydrogen production reaction.
As shown in Fig. 6, the absorption edges of pure rGO/MOF, pure $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ni}_{4}}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ were $316\mathrm{nm}$ and $437{\mathrm{nm}}$ , respectively, while the absorption edge of the composite photocatalyst $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ is between the absorption edges of rGO/MOF and the $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ . Obviously, the rGO/MOF photocatalyst with the introduction of $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ showed a significant red shift and displayed better light absorption properties compared with pure rGO/MOF. The calculated results by the Kubelka-Munk equation showed that the band gaps of pure $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{GO}}/$
MOF, pure $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ and $\Gamma\mathrm{GO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}(\$ $(20\%)$ are $3.92\mathrm{eV},2.84\mathrm{eV}$ and $3.47\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, which was contributing to that the graphene after recombined possessed a higher conductivity and was able to make the photogenerated electron-hole pairs can be effectively separated. The $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ composite photocatalyst had a stronger ability to capture visible light than the pure $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{GO}}/$ MOF photocatalyst, which was more conducive to the process of photocatalytic hydrogen production.
## 3.4. XPS analysis
In order to examine the valences of the elements in the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}\mathbf{G}\mathbf{O}/$ $\mathsf{M O F}/\mathsf{N i}_{4}\mathsf{S}_{3}$ $(20\%)$ composite photocatalyst, the XPS characterization is used and the results are shown in Fig. 7. Fig. 7(A) was the fine spectrum which showed that C, O, Ni, Zr and S elements coexisted in the $\Gamma{\sf G O}/{\sf M O F}/{\sf N i}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ composite photocatalyst. The binding energy of C 1s (Fig. 7B) was located at $284.79\mathrm{eV}$ and $286.5\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, and the formor peak can be attributed to carbon $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ in phenyl for the photocatalyst and the peak at $286.5\mathrm{eV}$ can be due to the $c{-}0$ in the graphene. Fig. 7(C) was the detailed spectrum of Zr. Two peaks have appeared after the curve fitting, and their binding energies were $182.24\mathrm{eV}$ and $185.05\mathrm{eV},$ , respectively. The peak at $182.24\:\mathrm{eV}$ can be attributed to the Zr $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ orbit, and the peak at $185.05\mathrm{eV}$ belonged to the Zr $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ orbit [29]. As can be seen from Fig. 6D, two peaks (531.96 eV and $530.32\mathrm{eV})$ resulted from the spectra of O1s, which belong to adsorbed hydroxyl and $z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ bonds, respectively [29–31]. The Fig. 4(E) was present the XPS analysis of $\mathsf{N i}\ 2\mathsf{p}$ . The binding energies at 875.56 and $857.90\mathrm{eV}$ were identified to $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ , and the other peaks at 870.46 and $853.09\mathrm{eV}$ were belong to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , which could be known that the Ni exists in the form of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ valence state. In Fig. 4 (F), the $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ and $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ with a characteristic binding energies of $162.95\mathrm{eV}$ and $161.59\mathrm{eV}$ was the core-level spectrum of the S 2p. And the other peaks of $\textsf{S}2\mathsf{p}$ which at around 168.84 and 170.06 eV were ascribed to the $S0_{4}^{2-}$ or $S0_{3}^{2-}$ , the reason of the formation maybe was the $S^{2-}$ of ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S}$ be oxidized in the process of reaction.
## 3.5. BET analysis
It is well-known that high specific surface area will produce more sites for the photocatalytic reaction because they can cause more adsorption of reactant molecules to facilitate the access of reactants. To compare the specific surface area differences of the samples, we characterized the specific surface area and pore size distribution of pure rGO/MOF and $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ samples by nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherm. As can be seen from Fig. 8(a), the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms of the samples are both subject to H3 hysteresis. Fig. 8(b) showed the pore size distribution curves calculated according to the Barrett-JoynerHalenda (BJH) method, which manifested that pure rGO/MOF and $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ photocatalysts mainly contain mesopores in the range of $_{0-50\mathrm{nm}}$ . From the specific surface area, pore volume and pore size parameter of rGO/MOF and $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ samples in Table 2, it can be seen that the $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ composite photocatalyst has a larger surface area, indicating that the introduced $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ may enter into the porous structure and channel of MOF, resulting in the decrease of pore volume and pore size. This was more conducive to the adsorption of dye molecules, thereby enhancing the photocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ composite photocatalyst.
## 3.6. Electrochemical characterization test
The transient photocurrent response curve further proved the electron transfer mechanism during the reaction process of dyesensitized rGO/MOF, $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ and $\Gamma{\cal G}0/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ . As shown in Fig. 9(a), it can be seen from the figure that the electron transfer efficiency of $\Gamma\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{EY-rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ was significantly enhanced compared to ITO/EY-rGO/MOF and $I\mathrm{T}0/\mathrm{EY-Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ . The above results showed that the existence of $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ could promote the transfer of electrons from excited state dye to rGO/MOF and then to ITO, leading to increasing the efficiency of photoinduced charge separation and photocatalytic hydrogen production.
In addition, we tested the electrochemical hydrogen evolution activity of $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ composite in TEOA solution by using linear sweep voltammetry technique and compared it with ITO/EY-rGO/MOF and $\mathsf{I T O/E Y–N i}_{4}S_{3}$ as the results shown in Fig. 9 (b). It can be seen from the figure that the cathode current corresponding to the reduction of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ to hydrogen is observed on the ITO conductive glass, and the current density is still low at a relative higher applied voltage. However, the reduction current density increased obviously in the same voltage range on the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{ITO/rGO}}/$ MOF $/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ electrode, which indicated that the addition of $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ can effectively improve the electron transfer efficiency of $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ $(20\%)$ photocatalyst and enhance the electrocatalytic hydrogen production activity.
## 3.7. Possible photocatalytic mechanism over rGO/MOF/Ni4S3
## 4. Conclusion
In this research, we successfully constructed a high-efficiency photocatalytic hydrogen production system without notable metals by hydrothermally adding the electron transfer agent $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ into the EY-sensitized rGO/MOF system. The results manifested that the photocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{MOF}/\mathrm{Ni}_{4}S_{3}$ photocatalyst was much higher than that of rGO/MOF photocatalyst. The rGO acts as electron-transfer mediator and $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ serves as $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -evolution active site. The $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ transferred electrons from the excited state dye to the rGO/MOF surface for proton reduction reaction. This kind of electron transfer mechanism can effectively improve the separation efficiency of photogenerated charge and the transfer efficiency of photogenerated electrons from excited state EY to rGO/MOF photocatalyst, leading to improving the efficiency of photocatalytic hydrogen production significantly. This research provided the experimental basis for the development and design of new nonprecious metal hydrogen production cocatalyst. The improvement in the efficient of photocatalytic hydrogen production will be the main research direction of our future work.
